\section*{The Kite Maker's Morning}

7CBEN06

Old Ismail spread the tissue paper across his workbench before the sun had
cleared the rooftops. Forty years he had cut and glued kites in this lane,
and still the first fold of a new morning made his fingers tingle.

"Chacha, will it fly today?" asked Ravi from the doorway. The boy came every
Saturday, and every Saturday he asked the same question.

"A kite always flies," said Ismail, not looking up. "The question is whether
the flyer listens to the wind."

Ravi edged closer. The workbench was a country of its own: bamboo slivers
sorted by length, pots of rice glue, and paper in twenty colours, thin
enough to read a newspaper through.

"Today," Ismail said, "you will make the kite yourself."

Ravi's ears went warm. He had watched a hundred kites take shape under the
old man's hands, but his own fingers felt like borrowed things. The first
spar he cut too short. The second split along its grain. Ismail said
nothing, only slid another sliver of bamboo across the bench.

By mid-morning a small, crooked, red-and-yellow kite lay finished between
them. It was not beautiful. One shoulder sat higher than the other, and the
tail was three rags too long.

"It will spin," Ravi said sadly.

"It will," agreed Ismail, "until you shorten the tail. A maker is not the
one who makes no mistakes. A maker is the one who knows which thread to
pull next."

They walked to the terrace. The wind came off the river in long, patient
breaths. The crooked kite climbed, wobbled, spun once — and then Ravi
pulled two rags from its tail and it steadied, a small red flame against
the white sky.

Ismail shaded his eyes and smiled. Forty years, and the first flight of a
new maker still felt like his own.

\section*{Working with the text}

1. Why do you think Ravi's ears "went warm" when Ismail asked him to make
the kite himself?
2. What did Ismail mean by "the flyer listens to the wind"?
3. The kite was "not beautiful", yet the morning ends happily. What does
the story suggest matters more than a perfect first attempt?
4. Pick two details that show Ismail is a patient teacher.

